After the SMS campaign is executed, the next step is to retrieve the delivery results.
In this step, we use a Python script to generate the SMS Delivery Report.
The script reads the campaign member file and checks the delivery response for each member by calling the SMS Manager API.
It filters the message records using the configured Program ID, Template IDs, and the campaign date range.
The final report shows whether a message was found, the API status, the carrier response status, and the related delivery timestamps.
This report is then used in the next step to load the SMS delivery information into the database table.”
Python script walkthrough
When you open the code, say:
“I’ll give a high-level overview of the Python script rather than going through every line.”
1. Configure the report filters
Show this section:
PROGRAM_FILTERS = "798365"
TEMPLATE_FILTERS = ["1790461", "1789223", "1788197"]

START_DATE_CST = "2026-07-09"
END_DATE_CST = "2026-07-18"
Say:
“At the beginning of the script, we configure the Program ID, the Template IDs, and the campaign date range.
These values ensure that the report includes only messages related to the campaign we are reviewing.”
You can add:
“The date range is inclusive, so both the start date and end date are included.”
2. Create a retry-enabled session
“The script creates a reusable API session with retry logic.
If the API has a temporary server or connection issue, such as a 502, 503, or 504 response, the script automatically retries instead of immediately failing.”
This is a good reliability point, but don’t spend too long on it.
3. Authenticate with the API
“Next, the Python script authenticates using the client ID and client secret and receives an access token.
The token is then included in the API headers for all phone and outbound-message requests.
If the token expires during the run, the script requests a new one.”
4. Read the member file
“The script reads the input CSV containing the members included in the campaign.
For each record, it reads fields such as GMPI ID, member phone number, first name, last name, and country code.”
5. Look up the Phone ID
“For each member, the script first calls the Phones API using the member’s phone number.
The purpose is to find the Phone ID stored in the SMS platform, because the outbound messages are retrieved using that Phone ID.
If no matching phone record is found, the report records the result as NOT_FOUND.”
6. Retrieve outbound messages
“Once the Phone ID is found, the script calls the Outbound Messages API.
It retrieves the message history for that phone and supports pagination, so it can process multiple pages if necessary.”
7. Apply the campaign filters
“The script then filters the outbound messages using three conditions:
the configured Program ID,
one of the configured Template IDs,
and the campaign delivery date range.”
Then explain the timezone logic:
“The API delivery timestamp is received in UTC. The script converts it to the America/Chicago timezone before applying the date filter.
This ensures that the report uses the campaign’s Central Time business dates.”
A small wording note: saying Central Time is more accurate than always saying CST, because the timezone library automatically accounts for daylight saving time.
8. Capture message status
“For every matching message, the script captures both the API message status and the carrier response status.
The carrier response is stored inside the service-response field, so the script parses that JSON value and extracts the status.”
Examples of fields captured:
GMPI ID
Phone ID
Phone Number
Member Name
Service Response Status
API Response Status
Deliver On
Created At
Updated At
Program ID
Template ID
9. Handle missing messages and errors
“If the phone exists but no matching outbound message is found for the selected campaign filters, the report records NO_MESSAGES.
If an exception occurs while processing a member, the error is captured in the report and written to the log instead of stopping the entire run.”
10. Generate the report
“After all members are processed, the script creates the SMS Response Report as a CSV file using the current run date.
It also creates a detailed log file that records authentication, phone lookups, message retrieval, filtered message counts, and any failures.”
Closing statement
“So, in summary, the Python script reads the campaign members, authenticates with the SMS platform, finds each member’s Phone ID, retrieves the outbound-message records, applies the campaign filters, captures the delivery statuses, and generates the final SMS Delivery Response Report.”
Simple flow to remember
Read Member File
      ↓
Authenticate with SMS API
      ↓
Find Phone ID
      ↓
Retrieve Outbound Messages
      ↓
Filter by Program, Template and Date
      ↓
Capture API and Carrier Status
      ↓
Generate Delivery Report CSV
One code detail to be aware of: although OUTPUT_CSV is passed into the class, the current script actually creates the file using:
dated_filename = f"SMSResponse_Report_{datetime.now().strftime('%Y%m%d')}.csv"
So the value assigned to OUTPUT_CSV is not currently used for the final filename.
